# .cursorrules — FurniTrack

You are working in a Flutter + Firebase project called FurniTrack, built for
a Kalvium (B.Tech CSE, Software Product Engineering) sprint-integration
assignment. Before making changes, read these files in this order — they
are the source of truth, not this file's summaries of them:

1. `PRD.md` — what the app must do and why, MVP vs stretch scope.
2. `architecture.md` — folder structure, data model, core flows, known gaps.
3. `design.md` — screen-by-screen UI intent and what not to change.
4. `phases.md` — the build order; work the next unfinished phase unless
   told otherwise.

## Non-negotiable constraints
- **Screens never call Firebase directly.** All Firestore/Auth/Storage
  access goes through `lib/services/*_service.dart`. If a screen needs new
  data access, add a method to the relevant service first.
- **Don't touch the conflict-check logic in `FirestoreService.createRental`**
  or the close-out logic in `closeRental` without re-reading
  `architecture.md`'s "Core flows" section — these two methods are the
  actual fix for the assignment's problem statement. A regression here is
  a regression in the whole point of the app.
- **`rentals.status` is the single source of truth** for whether an item is
  currently out. Don't derive "is it rented" from `deliveryEvents` or from
  `items.status` — read `architecture.md`'s "Why this shape" note before
  changing this.
- Every stream-backed screen must handle loading / error / empty states
  explicitly (see `OverdueItemsScreen` for the pattern). Don't ship a
  `StreamBuilder` that only handles the happy path.
- Every write path needs a validator (forms) or an explicit business-rule
  check (like the conflict check) before it touches Firestore — don't
  remove validation to make a demo path shorter.
- Firestore security rules (`firestore.rules`) must stay in sync with any
  new collection or role boundary you add. If you add a write path, add
  the matching rule in the same change.

## Code style
- Dart, null-safe, Flutter Material 3 (`useMaterial3: true`).
- One screen per file in `lib/screens/`; small reusable presentational
  widgets in `lib/widgets/`; plain data classes with `fromMap`/`toMap` in
  `lib/models/` — no business logic in models.
- Prefer `StreamBuilder` over one-off `get()` calls for anything the ops
  or crew UI treats as "current state" — this app's whole premise is live
  data, not polling.
- Keep new dependencies minimal; this is a student sprint project, not a
  production app — don't introduce a state-management library (Provider,
  Riverpod, Bloc) or routing package unless explicitly asked. `setState`
  and `StreamBuilder` are sufficient for this scope.
- Match the existing error-message tone: short, specific, written for a
  crew member standing at a customer's door (see `design.md` → Copy/tone).

## When adding a new phase feature (see `phases.md`)
1. Confirm which phase it belongs to and what's explicitly listed as its
   test/done condition.
2. Add/extend the service method(s) first, then the screen.
3. Update `firestore.rules` if a new collection or write path is involved.
4. Update `architecture.md`'s "Known placeholders / TODOs" section if you
   close out a listed gap, or add a new one if you knowingly leave
   something unfinished.

## Explicitly out of scope — don't build unless asked
- Payments/invoicing integration.
- Route optimization or crew scheduling/dispatch.
- Multi-city/multi-warehouse inventory.
- Social auth, password reset UI.
- Any state-management or routing package beyond Flutter's built-ins.

## Environment notes
- `lib/firebase_options.dart` is a placeholder that throws
  `UnsupportedError` — it must be regenerated with `flutterfire configure`
  against a real Firebase project before the app will run. Don't try to
  hand-write real-looking API keys into it.
- No item-seeding UI exists yet (that's Phase 2) — items must be added via
  the Firestore console for manual testing until then.
